Getting started with an f8 softcore on an iCEBreaker board


The hardware

The iCEBreaker is a popular cheap FPGA board, featuring an FPGA well-supported by a free toolchain. The board design is also free.
We use the iCEBreaker board with the LED/button PMOD at the PMOD2 connector (i.e. the default configuration of a freshly-bought iCEBreaker).


The toolchain 

* SDCC (4.5.0 or later)
* sv2v (0.0.12 or later)
* Icarus (12.0 or later)
* Yosys (0.38 or later)
* nextpnr (0.7 or later)
* IceStorm

Most of these are available as packages in OS distributions; if any of the tools is missing or tool old on in your distribution, compiling from source isn't hard.


The f8 softcore and sample programs

We will use a multicycle f8 implementation currently hosted at SourceForge, get via

svn checkout svn://svn.code.sf.net/p/sdcc/code/branches/f8/f8 f8

(or use git-svn if you prefer git)

First, run some pre-synthesis tests:

cd f8/hardware
make tests2

In the end, this should output a list of lines like "testsystem2: tests/clr/test2.log", but not "testsystem2: tests/clr/test2.log ERROR: TRAP".


Blinky

Ensure the LED/button PMOD is at the PMOD2 connector (i.e. the default configuration of a freshly-bought iCEBreaker).
Attach the FPGA board to a USB port providing sufficient power (it won't work on a bus-powered hub).
Create an FPGA image and write it onto the board via

make tests/cblink/icebreaker2_synth.bin
iceprog tests/cblink/icebreaker2_synth.bin

This should result in seconds being counted (0 to 31) on the 5 LED of the LED/button PMOD. The f8 can be reset via the button on the board. The source for the f8 program is tests/cblink/test.c: The timer is set up for a 1 kHz timer interrupt, and the interrupt handler increments the value on an I/O port once per second.


Hello, world!

Attach the FPGA board to a USB port providing sufficient power. Two serial devices will appear (e.g. visible in dmesg output). The first is used for writing the FPGA image onto the board, the second is used fo communication witht he f8. Use a terminal program (auch as gtkterm) configured for the second serial port at 9600 baud, 1 stop bit, no parity.
Create an FPGA image and write it onto the board via

make tests/chello/icebreaker2_synth.bin
iceprog tests/chello/icebreaker2_synth.bin

The text "Hello, world!" should be visible in your terminal program (and appear each time you reset the f8 via the button on the board). The source for the f8 program is tests/chello/test.c: The timer is set up for a 9.6 kHz timer interrupt, and the interrupt handler implements an UART in software, which i used to prove the C function putchar. This putchar is used for output to stdout by the ouput functions in the C standard library.


Dhrystone

Use the same setup as for "Hello, world!" above. Create an FPGA image and write it onto the board via

cd benchmarks
make dhrystone/icebreaker2_synth.bin
iceprog dhrystone/icebreaker2_synth.bin

At the time this tutorial is written, this will get us 592 Dhrystones/s on the f8 softcore running at 2 MHz. The f8 could actually run at higher clock speed, the 2 MHz were chosen to reuse the same code on multiple boards, since many boards support a clock that is a multiple of 2 MHz, including the iCEBreaker, which sues 12 MHz. An easy way to use a faster clock for the f8-based SoC is to adjust CLKDIV in icebreaker2.v.

